item.aggregated_output:
{"moveId":"pro-class-addresses-measurement","currentDescription":"Superdeterminism merits consideration because this theory class is expressly designed to replace unexplained measurement collapse with a physical account of definite outcomes.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder precisely establishes the proposal’s intended destination: a class of theories aimed at the measurement problem, rather than a single finished model already claimed to have solved it. Principal limitation: A declared design purpose supplies orientation, not evidence that any member can derive definite outcomes, recover quantum statistics, and remain compatible with established physics. Live burden: She must bridge the considerable distance between identifying a foundational problem and showing that statistical-independence violation offers a scientifically productive response rather than merely another possible framework."}
{"moveId":"con-remote-detector-causation-unclear","currentDescription":"Because only a tiny detector surface meets the particle, remote amplification degrees lack an evident local causal path to determining the microscopic outcome.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup converts a general complaint about incompleteness into a concrete physical question by separating the microscopic interaction surface from the apparatus that subsequently amplifies and displays its signal. Principal limitation: The distinction may be too sharp, since measurement and amplification could form one continuous many-body interaction whose relevant boundary cannot be fixed by spatial intuition alone. Live burden: He must show that detector-wide degrees cannot influence the interaction through an explicitly local evolution, or at least demonstrate why Hossenfelder’s promised dynamics face constraints stronger than ordinary underdevelopment."}
{"moveId":"pro-epistemic-update-lacks-physical-account","currentDescription":"If collapse only updates knowledge, it leaves unexplained what physically occurs locally; therefore a satisfactory measurement theory must supply an objective process compatible with relativity.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder cleanly distinguishes changing an observer’s information from explaining the physical process that produces a definite record, thereby locating a genuine explanatory burden for fundamental theory. Principal limitation: The move compresses the argument that this process must be local and compatible with general relativity, and it does not establish that epistemic interpretations cannot answer through a different ontology. Live burden: She must show why the missing physical description specifically favors superdeterministic dynamics, rather than collapse models, relational accounts, or other interpretations that also reject a primitive knowledge update."}
{"moveId":"pro-fundamental-theory-cannot-presuppose-observers","currentDescription":"Because quantum mechanics purports to describe nature’s constituents, macroscopic detectors, observers, and knowledge should emerge from its dynamics rather than appear among primitive assumptions.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder states an intelligible architectural standard for a fundamental theory: macroscopic measurement language should be derived from microscopic dynamics instead of entering as unexplained primitive vocabulary. Principal limitation: The reductionist standard is asserted rather than defended against approaches that treat relational structure, agents, or measurement contexts as legitimate features of physical description. Live burden: She still must demonstrate that a superdeterministic model can perform the required derivation and that its additional initial-condition correlations are less costly than the macroscopic concepts she criticizes."}
{"moveId":"pro-local-law-selects-detector-eigenstate","currentDescription":"Statistical-independence violation permits initial conditions and a local evolution law to converge on a detector eigenstate, thereby offering definite measurement outcomes without nonlocal dynamics.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder connects the abstract Bell assumption to the measurement problem by identifying the desired dynamical result: a local evolution from an initial state into a definite detector eigenstate. Principal limitation: Saying that statistical-independence violation permits such a law does not construct the law, demonstrate its stability, or explain the broader detector’s causal influence at the microscopic interaction. Live burden: Against Kastrup’s local-causation challenge, she must supply dynamics showing how relevant detector variables select outcomes while preserving quantum statistics, rather than infer explanatory achievement from formal compatibility alone."}
{"moveId":"pro-bell-locality-requires-si-violation","currentDescription":"Given Bell’s constraints, any theory reproducing observed correlations while retaining locality must violate statistical independence; superdeterminism names the research class taking that route.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder supplies the program’s central formal motivation as a conditional tradeoff: reproducing Bell correlations while preserving locality requires relaxing statistical independence. Principal limitation: The compressed statement leaves Bell’s other assumptions and the exact meaning of statistical independence unstated, so it establishes conditional availability rather than the physical plausibility of the chosen route. Live burden: She must explain why preserving locality deserves priority over competing responses and why the required correlations can arise through a credible, predictive mechanism rather than unrestricted adjustment of initial conditions."}
{"moveId":"con-si-supported-by-scientific-practice","currentDescription":"Statistical independence reflects science’s broad experience that changing an instrument setting does not thereby alter its object, so abandoning it carries substantial evidential cost.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup correctly insists that a successful methodological regularity should not be treated as a costless disposable premise, giving his resistance to superdeterminism a recognizably empirical basis. Principal limitation: The camera-aperture example describes ordinary instrument invariance and does not clearly instantiate Bell’s formal statistical independence between hidden states and setting choices. Live burden: He must establish that the historical regularity and the Bell condition are sufficiently analogous to transfer their evidential support, especially after Hossenfelder explicitly distinguishes the two notions."}
{"moveId":"pro-experimental-inference-assumption-dependent","currentDescription":"Kastrup’s experimental conclusion depends on inequalities derived using statistical independence; remove that premise, and the same observations no longer uniquely support his contextual interpretation.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder accurately separates observed data from the mathematical premises used to interpret them, directly challenging Kastrup’s claim that experiments themselves establish his preferred metaphysical conclusion. Principal limitation: Her phrase that experiments then say something entirely different remains broad, and she does not specify which alternative empirical conclusions survive once statistical independence is relaxed. Live burden: She must show that the relaxed inference yields constrained, independently testable models instead of merely preventing Bell experiments from excluding the superdeterministic option."}
{"moveId":"con-si-violation-needs-strong-rationale","currentDescription":"Although superdeterminism should remain researchable, asserting that nature violates historically successful statistical independence requires evidence substantially stronger than locality preference alone supplies.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup draws an excellent distinction between permitting inquiry and affirming that nature actually violates statistical independence, thereby setting a proportionate evidential threshold without excluding the program in advance. Principal limitation: The historical support remains only broadly characterized, and the move does not fully answer Hossenfelder’s contention that Bell independence differs from familiar independence used elsewhere. Live burden: He must specify what evidence would count as a sufficiently strong rationale and compare the cost of relaxing independence with the cost of relinquishing locality."}
{"moveId":"pro-locality-tradeoff-favors-si-violation","currentDescription":"Because preserving locality requires violating statistical independence, and general relativity is local yet must ultimately connect with quantum theory, Hossenfelder concludes this route is more promising.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder advances beyond logical possibility to a comparative rationale, arguing that locality’s central place in general relativity gives scientists a substantive reason to explore statistical-independence violation. Principal limitation: General relativity’s local structure does not by itself establish that locality must survive unchanged in quantum foundations, nor does the move comparatively price the unusual correlations her alternative requires. Live burden: She must demonstrate that preserving locality produces enough explanatory or predictive benefit to offset abandoning a condition central to conventional Bell inference and experimental design."}
{"moveId":"pro-measurement-setting-enters-evolution-law","currentDescription":"In Hossenfelder’s toy model, measurement context affects particle behavior because the chosen setting enters the evolution law, rather than through arbitrary downstream distortion.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder identifies a precise formal location for contextual dependence: the measurement setting enters the evolution law that determines what the particle does. Principal limitation: Merely locating the setting in an equation does not explain the physical origin of the required correlations, establish locality, or provide the law’s mathematical form within the exchange. Live burden: In answer to Kastrup, she must connect this formal dependence to a credible causal history and show that it constrains outcomes without allowing arbitrary accommodation of observed settings."}
{"moveId":"pro-hidden-information-may-be-observable","currentDescription":"Quantum randomness may reflect presently unknown information, and the historical label hidden does not entail permanent unobservability; such variables could eventually become measurable.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder usefully prevents a terminological shortcut by distinguishing variables absent from standard quantum theory from variables that are necessarily inaccessible in principle. Principal limitation: The possibility that unknown information may become observable supplies no independent evidence that it exists, determines quantum outcomes, or can be measured without disturbing the relevant system. Live burden: She must convert the modal suggestion into a specified model with observable consequences that distinguish hidden-information dynamics from ordinary quantum randomness."}
{"moveId":"con-unobserved-undefined-hidden-variables","currentDescription":"Hidden variables currently lack scientific standing, Kastrup argues, because they remain unobserved and the proposal under discussion fails to define what they are or do.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup identifies two scientifically important burdens—observability and theoretical specification—and connects them directly to whether hidden variables presently contribute more than a promissory label. Principal limitation: His universal claim that nobody has defined such variables is too broad and is directly answered when Hossenfelder names a toy model and specifies its random variables. Live burden: He must narrow the criticism from every hidden-variable theory to the physical adequacy, dynamics, and testability of the particular models actually defended."}
{"moveId":"pro-wrong-paper-model-exists","currentDescription":"Kastrup criticizes a paper that claimed no hidden-variable theory; Hossenfelder instead identifies a local deterministic toy model that explicitly defines and uses hidden variables.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder delivers a decisive source correction, distinguishing the path-integral paper Kastrup attacked from the separate local deterministic toy model that actually makes the hidden-variable claim. Principal limitation: Establishing that a relevant model exists and defines variables does not show that the model is physically adequate, empirically confirmed, or capable of resolving measurement beyond its limited purpose. Live burden: She must preserve the correction’s narrow force while demonstrating what the proper model proves and what remains unfinished, especially concerning dynamics and experimental discrimination."}
{"moveId":"con-quantumness-placeholder-not-theory","currentDescription":"Kastrup argues that an undefined quantumness term central to Hossenfelder’s path integral leaves that proposal a philosophical placeholder rather than a predictive theory.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup moves from general skepticism to a concrete textual criticism by naming the purported placeholder, explaining its minimization role, and identifying why an undefined central term limits prediction. Principal limitation: He targets a paper that did not claim to present the hidden-variable theory under debate, so even a valid criticism of that paper cannot establish that the relevant toy model lacks defined variables. Live burden: He must redirect the specification challenge toward the correct model and distinguish incompleteness within one proposal from absence of theoretical content across the research class."}
{"moveId":"pro-toy-model-defines-variables","currentDescription":"The limited toy model rebuts claims of conspiracy or nondefinition by specifying hidden variables as uniformly distributed random variables within the unit complex disk.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder answers the universal nondefinition charge with unusually concrete mathematical content, specifying both the variables’ domain and their uniform distribution while expressly limiting the model’s purpose. Principal limitation: A stipulated distribution in a toy construction does not establish that corresponding physical variables exist, recover full quantum phenomena, or enter a realistic detector evolution. Live burden: She must show how this possibility result informs a viable physical model and yields discriminating predictions rather than merely refuting the claim that no formal example can be written."}
{"moveId":"pro-repeated-measurements-address-state-ignorance","currentDescription":"The earlier proposal answers ignorance of the prepared hidden state by repeatedly measuring the same physical system, seeking correlations unavailable from separately prepared ensembles.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder supplies an operational response rather than denying the control problem, proposing repeated measurements on the same system to avoid dependence on unknown hidden-variable preparation across separate systems. Principal limitation: The exchange does not explain why repetition isolates hidden-state correlations despite measurement disturbance, temporal drift, or the statistical requirements needed to distinguish them from Born-rule behavior. Live burden: She must specify the observables, sequence, timescale, and adverse result that would count against the relevant model."}
{"moveId":"con-protocol-cannot-reset-hidden-state","currentDescription":"Cooling, simplifying, and rapidly retesting the apparatus reveal dependence on an unknown hidden state that may drift and cannot straightforwardly be restored between trials.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup reconstructs the protocol’s own precautions into a concrete experimental-control objection, showing why cooling, reduced degrees of freedom, and rapid repetition matter if hidden states drift. Principal limitation: Sensitivity to an uncontrolled state does not itself establish that repeated same-system measurements cannot extract the proposed signal or that quantitative bounds cannot constrain drift. Live burden: He must engage the correct noncommuting-variable protocol and demonstrate that its inference still depends on resetting, tracking, or assuming stability of the hidden state."}
{"moveId":"pro-noncommuting-measurement-protocol","currentDescription":"Kastrup’s drift dilemma targets a simplified experiment; Hossenfelder says the sufficient test repeatedly measures noncommuting variables on the same state without changing initial conditions.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder identifies a second consequential target mismatch, distinguishing the simplified later experiment from the same-state sequence of noncommuting measurements she regards as sufficient. Principal limitation: The assertion of sufficiency is not demonstrated; the exchange does not show how the protocol avoids disturbance, drift during the sequence, or ambiguity in interpreting a null result. Live burden: She must provide the predicted correlation structure, operational thresholds, and model-specific rejection condition that make the corrected protocol genuinely discriminating."}
{"moveId":"con-hidden-variables-lack-independent-need","currentDescription":"Unlike mass, charge, or angular momentum, hidden variables currently perform no independently indispensable causal work; removing them leaves established quantum predictions and explanations intact.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup offers a concrete criterion for scientific postulation by comparing hidden variables with familiar quantities whose removal visibly damages successful causal explanations. Principal limitation: Requiring a developing theoretical entity to be independently indispensable before testing it sets an unusually demanding standard and understates the explanatory work of addressing definite outcomes and locality. Live burden: He must show that the measurement problem supplies no genuine independent need, or that contextual alternatives answer it with fewer unsupported commitments than superdeterministic models."}
{"moveId":"pro-models-not-class-are-falsifiable","currentDescription":"Superdeterminism is a property shared by theories, not one theory; falsifiability properly applies to specific models that define hidden variables sufficiently to generate predictions.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder makes the debate’s cleanest methodological distinction, openly conceding that an entire theoretical class is not falsifiable while explaining how specified member models can be predictive and vulnerable to evidence. Principal limitation: The distinction does not guarantee that any presently available model has sufficiently fixed dynamics or adverse conditions to pass that model-level test. Live burden: She must identify which concrete model, variables, parameters, and outcomes provide the promised falsifiability, while resisting post-result movement among heterogeneous members of the class."}
{"moveId":"con-test-allows-drift-escape","currentDescription":"Too few repetitions can be declared inconclusive, yet extending each series increases elapsed time and permits null results to be excused by hidden-state drift.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup articulates a specific two-sided testing dilemma: short series lack statistical power, while longer series create more opportunity for the hidden initial state to drift between relevant comparisons. Principal limitation: The objection assumes that no model fixes sample-size and drift thresholds beforehand, and it initially addresses a simplified protocol rather than Hossenfelder’s claimed same-state noncommuting test. Live burden: He must show that the dilemma persists once the correct protocol and the numerical estimates from the earlier paper are incorporated."}
{"moveId":"pro-earlier-paper-supplies-thresholds","currentDescription":"Hossenfelder answers the specification challenge by citing a 2011 paper containing estimates for detector complexity, temperature, system size, and required repetition speed.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder answers every operational category Kastrup names with a specific earlier source and identifies the kinds of quantitative estimates it supplies: degrees of freedom, temperature, size, and timing. Principal limitation: No actual values, uncertainty ranges, predicted effect sizes, or rejection criteria appear in the exchange, so readers cannot determine whether the estimates make the experiment feasible or decisive. Live burden: She must connect those thresholds to a particular model’s adverse outcome and show that post hoc drift or apparatus complexity cannot absorb a null result."}
{"moveId":"con-protocol-operationally-under-specified","currentDescription":"Without numerical requirements for temperature, timing, observables, and permitted detector degrees, experimentalists cannot implement the proposal or identify a result that discriminates hypotheses.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup states the experimental burden with exceptional concreteness, asking exactly how cold, how rapid, which quantity, and which apparatus degrees an executable protocol requires. Principal limitation: The criticism is conditional on those answers being absent, and Hossenfelder immediately claims that an earlier paper provides numerical estimates across each requested category. Live burden: He must inspect those specifications and show they remain inadequate, model-dependent, or incapable of fixing adverse interpretation rather than treating their omission from the conversation as their omission from the proposal."}
{"moveId":"pro-detector-degrees-as-hidden-variables","currentDescription":"Hossenfelder’s developing model locates hidden variables in detector degrees of freedom distinct from the chosen measurement setting, giving them a candidate physical substrate.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder replaces an abstract hidden-variable label with a model-specific physical identification and carefully distinguishes detector degrees of freedom from the measurement setting itself. Principal limitation: Physical location alone supplies neither the variables’ relevant properties nor an evolution law showing how remote apparatus degrees determine the microscopic outcome. Live burden: She must demonstrate that these detector variables enter local dynamics, preserve established quantum statistics, and generate predictions different from standard quantum mechanics."}
{"moveId":"con-amplification-may-distort-outcome","currentDescription":"If downstream detector amplification determines rather than merely reports the microscopic result, it may manufacture or distort the very outcomes experiments are assumed to reveal.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup identifies a consequential reliability question: if later detector activity selects outcomes, the theory must explain why amplification remains faithful measurement rather than uncontrolled result production. Principal limitation: Outcome selection need not alter ensemble statistics or constitute distortion, and the claim that this would force rejection of all experiments greatly exceeds the conditional concern. Live burden: He must demonstrate that Hossenfelder’s detector variables change recorded distributions or sever their systematic relation to the measured observable, rather than merely determining individual outcomes within Born-rule constraints."}
{"moveId":"pro-evolution-law-still-incomplete","currentDescription":"Hossenfelder candidly concedes that her detector model remains incomplete because she has not determined exactly how detector degrees of freedom enter its evolution law.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder precisely identifies the unresolved mathematical component and does so without disguising a guiding idea as completed dynamics, giving the audience unusually reliable calibration of her model’s status. Principal limitation: The conceded gap is not peripheral; it is the mechanism needed to connect detector variables, locality, outcome selection, and empirical predictions. Live burden: She must explain why this incompleteness still permits the broader research route to be justified and prevent Kastrup from generalizing one model’s missing law to every superdeterministic approach."}
{"moveId":"con-complex-causal-chain-unsupported","currentDescription":"Embedding remote detector settings in microscopic evolution demands a highly nontrivial local causal chain; without a precise mechanism, the proposed dependence remains physically unsupported.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup accepts Hossenfelder’s formal clarification and presses the remaining issue at the correct point: writing the setting into a law does not explain the physical causal chain that realizes it. Principal limitation: The moon-camera analogy and language of incredulity provide intuition rather than evidence that no local common-cause history or detector dynamics could generate the dependence. Live burden: He must translate the demand for precision into identifiable physical constraints that the proposed model violates, rather than treating unfamiliarity as adverse evidence."}
{"moveId":"pro-detector-variables-preserve-born-mean","currentDescription":"Detector variables select individual outcomes without distorting measurement, Hossenfelder argues, because their ensemble distribution still reproduces quantum probabilities and the Born-rule mean.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder directly separates individual outcome selection from ensemble distortion, explaining that detector variables can determine a particular result while preserving the standard probability distribution and Born-rule average. Principal limitation: Preservation is asserted as a required model feature rather than derived from the still-incomplete dynamics, so compatibility has not yet become a physical explanation. Live burden: She must exhibit an evolution law or calculation that yields both definite outcomes and the Born distribution without inserting the desired statistics by construction."}
{"moveId":"pro-decoherence-scale-defines-detector","currentDescription":"A detector is pragmatically any system large enough to cause decoherence, though Hossenfelder explicitly denies that decoherence alone explains definite measurement outcomes.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder offers a physically motivated practical boundary for detector language while carefully separating that classificatory use of decoherence from the stronger claim that decoherence solves measurement. Principal limitation: “Large enough” remains approximate, and a pragmatic boundary does not determine when or how one outcome becomes actual or which microscopic degrees enter the proposed law. Live burden: Against Kastrup’s continuum of possible observers, she must make the decoherence threshold operational and connect it to the detector variables’ causal role."}
{"moveId":"con-experiments-undermine-noncontextual-realism","currentDescription":"Kastrup interprets decades of quantum experiments as rejecting preexisting standalone measured properties, thereby favoring contextual relations over hidden variables as the less costly route.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup defines the disputed realism clearly and supplies a substantive alternative interpretation, rather than merely objecting to superdeterminism without indicating how quantum results might otherwise be understood. Principal limitation: The move treats an assumption-mediated interpretation of Bell-related experiments as though the observations uniquely establish that metaphysical conclusion, without isolating realism from locality or statistical independence. Live burden: He must defend why contextuality is the best inference once Hossenfelder challenges the mathematical premises connecting data to his conclusion."}
{"moveId":"con-classical-carryover-selective","currentDescription":"Preserving classical-style standalone objects while discarding classical statistical independence is selectively inconsistent, Kastrup argues; the pro side must justify treating these inherited commitments differently.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup poses a useful comparative-consistency challenge, asking why Hossenfelder may retain objecthood and locality from classical experience while refusing similar evidential weight to statistical independence. Principal limitation: The argument assumes that these commitments have equivalent logical and empirical status, although a theory may consistently preserve one and revise another for distinct formal reasons. Live burden: He must show that Hossenfelder actually adopts the strong standalone realism attributed to her and that Bell independence travels with it rather than requiring separate assessment."}
{"moveId":"con-small-observers-support-contextuality","currentDescription":"If consciousness is not privileged, photons and modest decohering systems may both count as observers; Kastrup therefore treats physical quantities as relational rather than absolute.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup presses for a principled observer boundary and offers a coherent contextual alternative in which physical quantities belong to relations rather than possessing unconditional standalone values. Principal limitation: Rejecting consciousness as the boundary does not establish that every photon qualifies as an observer, nor does that equivalence alone entail a fully relational ontology. Live burden: He must define observer status independently, connect small-system experiments to the claimed ontology, and show why decoherence-based detector criteria are inadequate."}
{"moveId":"pro-one-incomplete-model-not-entire-class","currentDescription":"Her detector mathematics is unfinished, Hossenfelder concedes, but that model is only one superdeterministic approach; other programs posit different variables and mechanisms.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder combines a candid concession with a logically decisive scope correction: a missing mechanism in her detector proposal cannot automatically establish the same defect throughout a heterogeneous research class. Principal limitation: Invoking other models protects possibility but does not show that those alternatives possess adequate dynamics, evidence, or falsifiable predictions. Live burden: She must identify positive class-level reasons for continued investment without using diversity among unfinished proposals as insulation against model-specific failure."}
{"moveId":"con-superdeterminism-does-not-invalidate-science","currentDescription":"Kastrup rejects the claim that superdeterminism would invalidate science wholesale, narrowing his objection to whether this route presently has enough specific scientific support.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup explicitly rejects a common overbroad objection and credits Hossenfelder’s position, ensuring that the live dispute concerns present justification rather than the survival of scientific reasoning itself. Principal limitation: This scope clarification offers little affirmative support for his conclusion and answers a claim Hossenfelder did not need to press during this exchange. Live burden: He must use the narrowed terrain to show why particular models lack sufficient evidence, causal detail, or securely adverse tests without returning to blanket dismissals."}
{"moveId":"pro-locality-also-historically-successful","currentDescription":"Locality has its own dramatic scientific success, and Bell measurement independence differs from ordinary statistical independence; therefore Kastrup’s historical argument does not settle the tradeoff.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder answers Kastrup on both fronts, counterbalancing his historical appeal with locality’s success and challenging his transfer from ordinary statistical independence to Bell’s distinct formal condition. Principal limitation: She does not fully define the difference between those conditions, and saying they have nothing to do with each other risks overstating a distinction that still requires careful explanation. Live burden: She must compare the two historically supported commitments and show why sacrificing Bell independence is the more productive, empirically disciplined revision."}
{"moveId":"con-incompleteness-alone-not-disqualifying","currentDescription":"Kastrup grants that being unfinished is no scientific defect by itself, since every successful theory began as work in progress; stronger objections are required.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup expressly removes incompleteness alone from his case, fairly recognizing that productive theories and technologies necessarily pass through early stages before acquiring full specification and confirmation. Principal limitation: As a concession, the move supplies no affirmative reason that this particular program lacks justification beyond directing attention to its mechanism, evidence, and testability deficits. Live burden: He must show that the present gaps are materially worse than ordinary research immaturity and specify what progress would move superdeterminism across his threshold."}
{"moveId":"pro-experiments-must-guide-theory-building","currentDescription":"Because complete theory cannot be assembled without informative evidence, scientists should pursue discriminating experiments beyond repetitive Bell tests whose outcomes no longer guide model construction.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Hossenfelder supplies a credible methodological rationale for experimenting before theoretical completion: discriminating data can constrain the very dynamics that an immature research program has not yet fixed. Principal limitation: Experiments can guide theory only when existing hypotheses specify sufficiently distinct outcomes, so the principle cannot substitute for an executable protocol or preset rejection conditions. Live burden: She must show that her proposed measurements offer information beyond familiar Bell violations and can narrow model space even if they do not confirm her detector proposal."}
{"moveId":"con-no-experimental-reason-yet","currentDescription":"No independent experiment presently requires hidden variables or abandoning statistical independence, Kastrup concludes, particularly while the proposed entities still lack a complete causal account.","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup distills his case into two separate current deficits—no independent empirical motivation for hidden variables and no explicit theoretical mechanism sufficient to justify abandoning statistical independence. Principal limitation: The sweeping appeal to centuries of universal success blurs ordinary scientific independence with Bell’s formal condition and understates the measurement problem’s possible explanatory motivation. Live burden: He must define what experimental result would count for the program and assess Hossenfelder’s cited tests rather than infer lack of reason from their not yet being performed."}
